  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Minor snow impacts possible tonight on Lolo, Lookout and Marias\n  Passes\n\n- Anticipate cooler temperatures and periods of valley rain and \n  mountain snow through the weekend.\n\nA low pressure system digging into the Pacific northwest coastline\nwill continue to push a fetch of moisture into western Montana \nand north central Idaho this afternoon into tomorrow morning. The\nmain plume of moisture will generally focus north of Interstate 90\ntonight, with areas sough of Interstate 90 largely seeing\nprecipitation come to an end by this evening. Snow levels will \ncontinue to hover between 4000 and 5000 feet this afternoon, \nlowering to around 3500 feet overnight tonight. Passes such as\nLolo, Lookout and Marias will likely see a couple inches of\naccumulation, making for a period of slick road surfaces over\nnight. Valley locations near the Continental Divide, such as West\nGlacier, will likely see a brief period of snow as well, though\nsurface temperatures will make it difficult to accumulate. \n\nAs the low pressure system digs slightly to the south Thursday\ninto Friday, a warmer southwesterly flow sets up over the region.\nForecast models generally show drier conditions by Thursday\nafternoon into Friday, though cloud cover will linger. Seasonably\ncool temperatures will continue into the weekend.\n\nLater Saturday into Sunday, forecast models are beginning to show\nthe nearly stationary low pressure system along the west coast\nfinally giving way and moving eastwards into central Idaho and\nsouthwest Montana. Another push of moisture will bring a focus of\nprecipitation into the region, generally along and south of\nHighway 12. There is still a wide spread in potential snow levels\n(between 5000 to 7000 feet is the most likely range) but there is\nrelatively high confidence that mountains and high mountain passes\n(Lost Trail, Homestake, MacDonald, Bannock) will see accumulating\nsnow and periods of slick conditions by Sunday morning.",
